# Context

## Background
A developer builds ohya from a tree with uncommitted changes and
restarts the binary many times between two commits. Each build has the
revision of HEAD. The mcp_build check compared the revision only, so
it said "this build" for an ohya mcp process that ran an earlier
build of that revision.

## Why This Way
- The snapshot of an instance holds the revision and the uncommitted
  flag of its build (observe.Instance). A pair identifies a committed
  build. It does not identify an uncommitted build: two of them at one
  revision can hold different code, and the check cannot know.
- For that pair the check reports a warning and says that the process
  can also be the same build. The other choice, silence, hides the
  common case of a developer who rebuilt without a commit.
- A build is dirty when git status reports any change, also an
  untracked file. So a build from a clean checkout with one stray
  file is "uncommitted" here. That is a false warning at most.
- buildIdentity holds the conversion that doctor and setup share. The
  comparison with "true" is the one of startTelemetry, which writes
  the flag into the snapshots.

## Gotchas
- A test binary carries no build stamp: Identity returns "unknown".
  The line of buildIdentity that reads the stamp has no test. The
  runtime struct of each command overrides revision and flag.
- 12 mutation checks failed a test: the revision-only rule, each flag
  of the rule, each term of the alike note, each part of the build
  name, the flags that doctor passes, and the guard for an unknown
  revision.
- An uncommitted doctor never produces the ok finding: each process
  at its revision differs in the flag or is an alike build.

